# Source: built from HoareLogic.pdf, "A Quick Example" (the program abs), and exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 a).
# The sheet has the postcondition y >= 0 only. The postcondition here also fixes the value of y,
# as in "A Quick Example" (where the first connective is printed as || by mistake; it is && here).
id: absolute-value
title: Absolute value
topic: Conditional
level: 1
source: Built from HoareLogic.pdf, "A Quick Example", and exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 a
tags: conditional, wp, consequence

task:
  The program stores in $y$ the absolute value of $x$. The postcondition
  $Q$ does not only ask for $y >= 0$: it also gives the value of $y$ in
  each case, $y = x$ when $x >= 0$ and $y = -x$ when $x < 0$.

  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for $Q$: first the
  weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the conditional.
  Then use the consequence rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

given:
  Condition B: $x < 0$

pre: true
post: y >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> y = x) && (x < 0 ==> y = -x)

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if x < 0 then {
    {R1}
    y := -x           // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    y := x            // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch y := -x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment
  expression ($-x$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := -x, Q)
  = (y >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> y = x) && (x < 0 ==> y = -x))[y <- -x]
  = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x) && (x < 0 ==> -x = -x)   // every y is replaced by -x
  = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x) && (x < 0 ==> true)      // -x = -x is true
  = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x) && true                  // A ==> true is true
  = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x)                          // A && true is A
  = x <= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x)                           // multiply -x >= 0 by -1, which reverses it
  = x <= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> x = 0)                            // -x = x holds exactly when x = 0
  = x <= 0                                                  // with x <= 0, x >= 0 holds only for x = 0, so the implication is true
set: R1 = x <= 0

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch y := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment
  expression ($x$) in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(y := x, Q)
  = (y >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> y = x) && (x < 0 ==> y = -x))[y <- x]
  = x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> x = x) && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)      // every y is replaced by x
  = x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> true) && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)       // x = x is true
  = x >= 0 && true && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)                    // A ==> true is true
  = x >= 0 && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)                            // A && true is A
  = x >= 0                                                  // with x >= 0, the left side x < 0 is false, so the implication is true
set: R2 = x >= 0

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x < 0$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x < 0 : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x < 0) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if x < 0 then y := -x else y := x, Q)
  = (x < 0 ==> x <= 0) && (!(x < 0) ==> x >= 0)    // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = (x < 0 ==> x <= 0) && (x >= 0 ==> x >= 0)      // !(x < 0) is x >= 0
  = (x < 0 ==> x <= 0) && true                     // A ==> A is true
  = true && true                                   // x < 0 implies x <= 0
  = true                                           // true && true is true
set: R3 = true

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: true ==> true
why:
  $R3$ is $true$, and every formula implies $true$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The then branch needs $x <= 0$ and the else branch needs $x >= 0$.
  Each of these is implied by the condition under which the branch runs,
  so the program computes the absolute value from every initial state.
